scenario the receiver publishes a protection level and then disowns it
describe A protection level is the receiver's own statement about how wrong it
describe could be. Two failures are possible and they are different: a bound so
describe wide it is useless, and a bound the receiver marks invalid — which is
describe the receiver saying it cannot bound its own error at all.

at 0
  fix 3d
  pos 5000000 10000000
  alt 42000
  acc 3500
  sats 11 14
  jam none
  spoof none
  plevel valid 8000 12000
  expect trust trusted
  expect no-reason protection-level-exceeded
  expect no-reason protection-level-invalid

# Still valid, and now wider than anything worth navigating by.
at 3000
  fix 3d
  pos 5000010 10000010
  alt 42000
  acc 9000
  sats 9 14
  jam none
  spoof none
  plevel valid 120000 180000
  expect reason protection-level-exceeded
  expect no-reason protection-level-invalid
  # Recorded, and on its own not enough to move the state: a wide bound is
  # worth 25 against a degrade threshold of 30. That is deliberate — a bound
  # that is merely wide is a caveat about precision, and it becomes a caveat
  # about trust the moment anything else agrees with it.
  expect trust trusted

# And now it will not stand behind the number at all.
at 6000
  fix 3d
  pos 5000020 10000020
  alt 42000
  acc 11000
  sats 8 14
  jam none
  spoof none
  plevel invalid 0 0
  expect reason protection-level-invalid
  expect no-reason protection-level-exceeded
  # A receiver that will not bound its own error is worth 30, and 30 is the
  # threshold. "I cannot tell you how wrong I am" is a different statement from
  # "I could be quite wrong".
  expect trust degraded
